## Challenges in crystalline CPs and MOFs and opportunities for amorphous analogues
The periodic and ordered structure of crystalline coordination polymers (CPs) and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) endow the materials with ultrahigh porosity and mechanical rigidity. This rigidity and accompanying brittleness make it challenging to process crystalline CPs and MOFs into functional macroscopic systems (such as thin films and monoliths), often leading to the loss of accessible pores. Moreover, many crystalline CPs and MOFs are synthesized at high temperature and pressure with organic solvents; this is an energy-intensive process, and the use of solvents might present environmental risks and affect downstream applications.By contrast, the flexible and disordered nature of amorphous CPs (aCPs) and amorphous MOFs (aMOFs) gives rise to mechanical and chemical robustness. This robustness and accompanying processability make it easier to transform aCPs and aMOFs into functional macroscopic systems (for example, thin films, monoliths and hydrogels) with the added benefits of facile cargo loading and tunable release. Moreover, many aCPs and aMOFs are synthesized in ambient and aqueous conditions; therefore, they are often more environmentally friendly than their crystalline counterparts and are generally stable in water.
