## Direct synthesis of aCPs and aMOFs
Although the amorphization of crystalline CPs is possible\(^{23,32}\), going straight from precursors to aCPs during synthesis offers advantages in terms of milder synthesis conditions, a wider range of applicable building blocks and the formation of products with enhanced physicochemical properties. Various approaches can be used to generate coordination defects, which minimize the kinetic or thermodynamic energy of the metal–organic complexes at the particle–solution interface and, thus, prevent crystallization (either permanently or temporarily) during CP formation\(^{33,34}\). Thus, most aCPs are spherical when synthesized in solution and can be highly adherent when synthesized at interfaces, which can have benefits in applications ranging from drug delivery to photonics\(^{35}\). In the following sections, we discuss the different approaches used to directly synthesize aCPs from precursors under ambient conditions.
## Flexible and/or asymmetric ligands
Natural and synthetic flexible and/or asymmetric ligands with functional groups that can strongly coordinate to metal ions (such as phenolic and phosphonate moieties) can be used to form aCPs under ambient conditions, as the intramolecular rotation of the ligands, lack of symmetry planes and the relatively strong cumulative chelation strength of the functional groups can prevent or slow crystallization\(^{36,37}\) (Fig. 2). With the direct synthesis approach, water is often used as the solvent, which requires the interaction between the metal and ligand to be comparable to or stronger than the interactions between the metal and water, and the resulting aCPs are therefore often stable in a wide range of polar and nonpolar solvents, in contrast to many classes
of crystalline and amorphized CPs, which are not stable in polar solvents (such as water)16. Moreover, the radius of the metal is not a major concern when directly synthesizing aCPs, unlike when synthesizing crystalline coordination materials, given that the size of the ion can change or interfere with the crystal structure. Therefore, a wide range of metals can be used with flexible and/or asymmetric ligands without notable changes to the experimental protocol38–40.
Nucleic acids are flexible biopolymers with a strong affinity for metal ions and can be readily used to prepare aCPs; indeed, most of the metal–nucleic acid CP literature relates to aCPs41,42. Owing to the flexibility of the ligands, synthesizing nucleic-acid-based crystalline CPs is challenging and requires precise synthesis conditions, whereas aCPs can be readily prepared by mixing the precursors in water at room temperature43,44. Although different nucleotides share many features, only nucleotides with certain structures can form CPs with specific metals that are easy to reduce, such as gold45. Moreover, the metal, nucleotide and number of phosphates on the nucleotide have a notable role in determining the type of aCP formed (such as particle or gel), their subsequent interaction with other molecules43 and their optical, magnetic and catalytic properties44,46.
Amino acids also have an affinity for metal ions through their termini and often through their side chain. Furthermore, peptides and proteins can adopt various conformations and therefore tend to form aCPs rather than crystalline CPs upon chelation with metals47,48. Synthetic approaches have been developed to tune the amino acid sequence and to incorporate disulfide bridges (from peptides) into the final aCPs, which can allow for the formation of redox-responsive aCPs that disassemble in the presence of chelating agents49. Moreover, self-assembled peptides with complex architectures, such as disks, can be further assembled into hollow spheres through the addition of metal ions50 without the loss of structure, which implies that their function is also maintained.
Many hydroxy-rich biopolymers can also be used to synthesize aCPs, with representative ligands including chitosan, alginate, cyclodextrins, phenolics and cellulose37,51–53. The resulting aCPs tend to be highly biocompatible and biodegradable and, owing to the incorporation of protonatable chelators, undergo reversible interactions in response to external stimuli38. aCPs constructed from hydroxyrich small natural molecules (such as gallic acid and tannic acid) can have metal-dependent disassembly profiles owing to the multivalent interactions of the ligands and can be covalently crosslinked to change their stability and porosity30,38. Cellulosic materials and chitosan are also commonly used to form aCPs owing to their large size and flexibility, which make the formation of crystalline CPs more challenging54,55.
Phosphonates (natural or synthetic) are a class of small molecules that can have short-range order, but it is challenging to order them on the longer length scales needed to generate crystalline materials owing to their strong and fast chelation56, although microwave synthesis can facilitate crystallization57. Phosphonates are thus regularly used to directly synthesize aCPs52. Surfactants and evaporation-induced self-assembly can be used to facilitate the formation of phosphonate-based aCPs58,59, which can be synthesized in different shapes, such as 2D materials60 and hollow capsules61.
In addition to natural ligands, a wide range of synthetic flexible and/or asymmetric small-molecule ligands can be used to directly synthesize aCPs. For example, replacing a rigid ligand that forms crystalline CPs with a chemically similar flexible ligand can result in aCPs35 and, by mixing asymmetric and symmetric ligands, aCPs and semi-crystalline
## a Flexible and/or asymmetric ligands
and crystalline CPs can be formed, depending on the ratio between the ligands62. Flexible ligands with different symmetries, geometries and arm numbers have been used to form different tubular aCPs, demonstrating that ligand morphology influences particle shape, even in amorphous materials63. Interestingly, small rigid ligands with specific geometries can also be used to form aCPs through self-limited growth, as the rigid structure of the ligand impairs subsequent chelation steps and epitaxial growth64.
Flexible polymeric synthetic ligands have a high degree of chemical tunability and can be used to form crystalline CPs or aCPs65, depending on the spacer length between the chelating groups on the side chain. Specifically, spacers that are too short or too long lead to amorphous materials, which generally lack the porosity of some crystalline polymer-based CPs66. Alternatively, chelating groups can be added to the termini of polymers to preserve the functionality of the polymers themselves, which can result in the formation of highly stable amorphous materials67.
In summary, using flexible and/or asymmetric ligands is a common approach to directly synthesizing aCPs under mild, ambient conditions, although there are some limitations in terms of the building blocks as many of the reported ligands conventionally used to synthesize CPs are rigid and symmetrical. Nevertheless, the flexibility and/or asymmetry of the ligands means that multiple functionalities can be incorporated into the building blocks before synthesis. Finally, it is noted that although many flexible and/or asymmetric ligands
readily lead to aCPs, there is also interest in crystallizing CPs composed of such ligands65.
## Templates and seeds
Templates and seeding agents can be used to induce the formation of aCPs by locally increasing the concentrations of the metal and/or organic ligand68 (Fig. 3). This approach, therefore, allows for synthesis at lower concentrations of the precursors (up to several orders of magnitude)55 and in different solvents (such as water) than commonly used for crystal synthesis. However, the formation of aCPs on templates generally requires interactions between the ligand and the surface of the template, and therefore the chemical properties of the ligand must
be taken into consideration30. By contrast, seeding agents generally only need to act as a sink for the metals30.
Templates can be used to concentrate ligands that form metal–organic complexes, and the use of functional or cargo-loaded templates also offers a high degree of control over the functionality of the resultant core–shell materials69–71. For example, some adhesive ligands, such as phenolics and phosphonates, can adsorb onto templates while chelating metals. These concurrent processes locally increase the concentrations of both precursors, which lead to the formation of aCP coatings, sometimes in a matter of seconds30. Strongly chelating organic ligands and aqueous synthesis conditions are generally used with the templating method, leading to increased water stability of the resultant \(\mathsf{C P S}^{72-74}\) . The strength of the coordination interactions within aCP coatings is comparable to that between the metal ions and water molecules; thus, the CPs are sufficiently robust to avoid disassembly in the presence of water and, therefore, can be used for a wide range of aqueous-based applications (such as drug delivery). Sacrificial templates can be selectively removed in specific solvents and can therefore be used to form hollow and mesoporous aCP replicas of different template geometries30,69 (Fig. 3a,b).
Phenolics strongly adhere to many surfaces, which allow for the formation of surface-confined aCPs, termed metal–phenolic networks (MPNs), on various nanoscopic and macroscopic templates30,69 (Fig. 3c). The ability to form MPNs using different metals and ligands enables the porosity and responsiveness to external stimuli (such as pH, solvent and temperature) to be tuned30,38,69. The diverse interactions between the phenolic ligands themselves, as well as between the ligands and the metals and templates (such as hydrogen bonding, π interactions, electrostatics, chelation and hydrophobic effects), facilitate the rapid formation and deposition of MPNs33. Moreover, these diverse interactions impart water stability to MPNs, and the disassembly properties can be tuned through judicious choice of the metal ions. More than 8,000 phenolic molecules with different functions (for example, anticancer and neuroprotective effects) have been discovered in nature to date, and given the synthesis of phenolic polymers75, the field of MPNs should continue to grow.
Phosphonate-based aCPs can coat various substrates, and the resultant films are generally more stable than phenolic-based coatings when using the same metal owing to phosphonates being stronger electron donors72. The rapid chelation and formation of metal–phosphonate complexes means that phosphonates readily form amorphous structures when interacting with metals in water, particularly for trivalent and tetravalent metals76, and the strong hydrogen bonding between the phosphonic acid groups and water molecules endows phosphonate-based aCP coatings with high water stability and superhydrophilicity72.
Notably, the use of templates can often lead to aCPs with ligands that might otherwise yield crystalline CPs in the absence of templates68,77. This outcome is probably due to the metal–organic coatings being too thin for sufficient rearrangement to produce long-range order and/or the high curvature of the templates78,79. For example, when palladium nanoparticle templates were used, a transition from an amorphous coating to a crystalline coating was seen during zinc-based CP film growth over \(4\,\mathrm{h}\), which is directly correlated with the thickness of the coating increasing over time80. The surface chemistry of the template is also important in achieving uniform coatings: surface modification, such as through functionalization with carboxylate groups or coating with chitosan, can facilitate the deposition of uniform aCP coatings81.
