Similar to the templating of aCPs, seeding agents such as synthetic polymers and biomolecules can locally increase the concentration of precursors to rapidly generate amorphous materials at low precursor concentrations and in aqueous conditions that are generally not favourable for preparing large complexes (Fig. 3d,e). Seeding agents can therefore expedite the complexation process, hinder crystallization and produce coordination defects77 (Fig. 4). Engineering defects into CPs, whether with seeding agents or otherwise, allows for control of the amorphousness, as increasing the size and number of defects decreases the crystallinity. Synthetic polymers such as polyvinylpyrrolidone and biomolecules ranging from cellulose nanocrystals to chitosan and proteins have been used as seeds for amorphous coordination materials with different ligands, often leading to enlarged pores and sometimes higher surface areas compared with coordination materials prepared without seeding agents82-85.In summary, templates and seeding agents are commonly used for generating aCPs from building blocks that are routinely used to synthesize crystalline materials. This approach can allow for synthesis in aqueous, ambient conditions, as well as the facile loading of functional cargo by using the cargo as templating agents or seeds38,75,82. Moreover, multiple different forms or sizes of cargo can be readily integrated during synthesis, which would normally prove challenging for crystalline CPs owing to their specific and narrow pore sizes. Notably, sacrificial templates can enable the generation of complex and hierarchical aCPs from relatively straightforward synthesis approaches. Additionally, the use of templates and seeding agents can decrease the quantity of
