## Synthesis conditions
Specific synthesis conditions are important in controlling the formation of aCPs versus crystalline CPs. Conditions that cause amorphous particles or precipitates to form from precursors commonly used to prepare crystalline materials can be harnessed to directly synthesize aCPs and aMOFs (Fig. 5). Overall, the solvent, reaction time and environmental conditions (pH, temperature, pressure and ionic strength) during synthesis, together with the ligand-to-metal ratios and concentrations, and even the metal salts used as precursors, are interrelated in dictating whether aCPs or crystalline CPs form86 and allow for control of the final structure and properties (that is, morphology, crystallinity and porosity) of CPs.
