whereas ambient conditions are regularly used for a MOFs<sup>98</sup>. Generally, higher temperatures lead to higher crystallinity or expedite crystallization<sup>99,100</sup>. aCPs synthesized with flexible ligands, such as phenolics and phosphonates, can convert into crystals under appropriate solvothermal treatment<sup>101,102</sup>, but often only specific pH and concentrations yield pure crystals, with the exact conditions required for crystallization dependent on the metal used<sup>103,104</sup>.
Many aCPs can be directly synthesized in water even though water interacts strongly with the metal ions<sup>30,72</sup>. This strong interaction can lead to unsaturated metal sites that propagate defects and decrease long-range order in the complexes<sup>33</sup>. Alternatively, amorphous materials can be precipitated by dissolving metal–organic complexes in a solvent in which they are highly soluble, followed by rapid introduction of a solvent in which they are insoluble<sup>25</sup>. Interestingly, organic solvent additives can inhibit or promote the formation of crystals depending on the system studied, and aCPs can sometimes be produced by adding a co-solvent to water<sup>105</sup>. In addition, for the same precursors, the use of different solvents can produce crystalline or amorphous materials. For example, in the synthesis of hollow MOF nanocages, switching from \(N,N\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) to \(N,N\)-dimethylacetamide led to the formation of a MOFs<sup>106</sup>. Moreover, in the synthesis of MOF nanopolyhedra, using triethylamine as an additive to DMF led to a MOF particles, whereas adding both triethylamine and hexane to DMF led to crystalline MOF particles<sup>107</sup>. A dynamic solvent-induced crystallization–amorphization process has been observed in the synthesis of infinite CP microparticles: solvent exchange between pure pyridine and pyridine mixed with another solvent resulted in the dissolution or formation of CPs, respectively, and could also be used to transition between aCPs and crystalline CPs, depending on the choice of the additional solvent<sup>25,108</sup> (Fig. 5). aCPs can also be synthesized in one solvent and then crystallized in another solvent upon subsequent heating<sup>109</sup>, which highlights the importance of aCPs as intermediates to crystalline materials.
The concentration, ratio and type (such as choice of metal salt) of precursor all have a role in determining the crystallinity of CPs. Lowering the ligand-to-metal ratio can cause irregular coordination when using rigid ligands, as the excess metal ions disrupt the long-range ordering of the coordination network and cause defects, thereby leading to aCP formation<sup>82</sup>. The irregular coordination can lead to relatively large and inhomogeneous pores in amorphous materials compared with the pores observed in crystalline CPs or amorphized CPs. Similar trends are observed with biomolecule-seeded materials, for which lower ligand-to-metal ratios result in highly stable aCPs, whereas higher ratios produce amorphous materials that readily dissolve and reform as crystals<sup>82</sup>. Additionally, the concentration of the seed itself can play a part in determining the amorphous nature of the final material, as higher concentrations of biomolecule seeds can lead to higher temperatures being required to crystallize the product because of the defects that can occur with seeding agents<sup>84</sup>. Similarly, fixed ratios, but lower concentrations of precursors, have been used to produce aMOFs when templated on nanowires<sup>78</sup>. Excessively high concentrations of either or both precursors can lead to fast chelation and crosslinking of metal–organic complexes and impede their further rearrangement into an ordered structure, resulting in aCPs<sup>110</sup>. Collectively, these examples highlight that the design space for aCPs is larger than that of crystalline CPs.
The formation of amorphous materials can also be promoted by the use of mixed metals, owing to mismatches in their radii and valency causing defects and hindering long-range order, even when
using solvothermal methods<sup>111</sup>. Additionally, specific organic metal salt precursors can be used during synthesis to stabilize the metal ion and to prevent oxidation or reduction and modulate the formation process<sup>112</sup>. For this reason, metal acetate salts are often used for synthesizing aCPs because they dissolve at higher pH and relative basicity compared with many other metal salt precursors, which allows for synthesis to occur at higher pH, and acetate can act as a modulating counterion for regulating the ordered coordination modes between the metal ions, the solvent and competing organic ligands<sup>105</sup>.
In summary, controlling the synthesis conditions offers a route to engineering aCPs. It is often easier to synthesize aCPs than it is to synthesize their crystalline counterparts, and therefore less troubleshooting is generally required when targeting amorphous materials.
## Choosing whether to crystallize CPs
When designing CPs for various applications, an important question is whether crystalline or amorphous materials should be used. This choice determines not only the selection of the building blocks but more importantly properties of the CP, including those related to their application, such as water stability and ability to coat substrates (Table 1). For example, density functional theory calculations have indicated that some amorphous zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) and ZIF analogues have higher dielectric constants than their crystalline counterparts<sup>113</sup>. Experimentally, a difference in the electric properties has been found for other MOF types: amorphous bimetallic terephthalate MOFs had lower (more desirable) charge transfer resistance (\(\sim\!2.3\,\Omega\)) than their crystalline counterparts (\(\sim\!4.8\,\Omega\))<sup>114</sup>. It is therefore crucial to weigh up the pros and cons of each form of a CP, and
ideally amorphous and crystalline forms should be characterized when both are synthesized (whether by design or chance), as experimental comparisons between compositionally identical (or nearly identical) crystalline CPs and aCPs are still limited.
A class of aCPs regularly compared against their crystalline counterparts are phosphonate CPs owing to the relative ease of making both types of coordination materials. This comparison allows for a better understanding of structure–function relationships. For example, iron–nickel-based phosphonate aCPs achieved nearly \(100\%\) photocatalytic water oxidation versus only \(40\%\) for their crystalline counterparts\(^{102}\). Similarly, cobalt–iron-based phosphonate aCPs exhibited better catalytic performance than their crystalline forms, as observed by the lower overpotential (278 versus \(322\,\mathrm{mV}\), respectively)\(^{101}\), which is probably due to the abundant surface defects and lattice dislocations. Besides phosphonate CPs, the solvothermal crystallization of aMOFs can allow for useful comparisons. For example, zirconium-based aMOFs had considerably lower overpotentials in oxygen evolution reactions than their crystallized forms\(^{98}\).
Moreover, enzyme-loaded directly synthesized aMOFs exhibited 5–20 times higher catalytic activity than chemically identical enzyme-loaded crystalline MOFs, because the larger pore size in the amorphous materials resulted in a lower diffusion barrier, thus facilitating the diffusion of substrates\(^{98}\) (Fig. 4). Similarly, with amorphous iridium-based CP coatings, access to the underlying template was retained and, therefore, the catalytic ability of the template material was preserved\(^{79}\), which was not the case for analogous crystalline coatings. Although directly synthesized aCPs regularly differ in their performance from crystalline CPs, they can also differ from CPs amorphized from a crystalline state, especially in regard to pore access and catalytic performance\(^{82,102}\). For example, aMOFs prepared through direct synthesis using seeds featured expanded pores and a lower diffusion barrier\(^{82}\) than the same aMOFs prepared by amorphization of crystalline MOFs\(^{115}\).
The physical structure of CPs can also have a notable role in determining potential applications. For example, owing to the reduction in scattering losses because of the elimination of hard edges, spherically shaped aMOF microcavities prepared from flexible ligands exhibited superior microlaser performance than their crystalline counterparts prepared from rigid ligands\(^{35}\). In another example, the packing density of MPNs was controlled through various routes, such as the use of oxidizing metals, high concentration of precursors and different solvents or pH, to tune the pore size between \(<1\) and \(>100\,\mathrm{nm}\) (refs. \(^{70,116,117}\)) and thus modulate the permeability, offering a higher degree of control compared with crystalline phenolic MOFs\(^{118}\). Crystalline and amorphous forms of the same MOF can have similar pore volumes and surface areas\(^{109}\), although aMOFs with flexible ligands might have lower surface areas than their rigid-ligand crystalline counterparts owing to pore collapse\(^{62}\). Nevertheless, aCPs prepared from high seed concentrations of polyvinylpyrrolidone exhibited surface areas as high as \(\sim500\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is comparable to that of many crystalline CPs and MOFs, and showed higher anionic dye adsorption (\(\sim1,000\,\mathrm{mg}\) of dye per gram of CP) than many crystalline materials owing to a combination of more unsaturated metal sites and better accessibility to hydrophobic regions\(^{119}\). Other aMOFs have exhibited a higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) absorption capacity (0.8 mmol \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) per gram) than their crystalline form (0.1 mmol \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) per gram)\(^{120}\) or have an adsorption capacity for lanthanides and other heavy metals that is nearly twice that of the crystalline forms\(^{100,121,122}\).
Generally, compared with crystalline CPs, aCPs have a wider pore-size distribution with randomly interconnected pores; thus, processing
Generally, compared with crystalline CPs, aCPs have a wider pore-size distribution with randomly interconnected pores; thus, processing methods are less likely to completely close, collapse or fill all the accessible pore volume. Control of the pore size and geometry during processing is key to achieving crystalline CPs with a high surface area, and improvements have led to an increase in the surface area of three orders of magnitude over the past two decades\(^{1}\). Pore size is often controlled over only a single order of magnitude (for example, between 1 and \(\sim10\,\mathrm{nm}\)), as the pore size of crystalline materials is generally controlled by varying the ligand size or by using templates\(^{123,124}\). By contrast, the pore size of aCPs can be additionally tuned by, for example, controlling the synthesis conditions and adding removable templates\(^{69}\), allowing for control of the pore and void sizes over at least three orders of magnitude\(^{70,116}\). However, although the potential range of pore sizes is much larger, the ability to engineer narrow pore distributions can prove challenging in amorphous materials.
Although aCPs show promising performance in specific applications, there are still notable gaps in understanding their limitations. For example, aCPs that are responsive to force and temperature can be formed from flexible dendritic pyridyl ligands\(^{125}\), but their integration into devices is yet to be achieved. Therefore, comparative studies between crystalline CPs and aCPs are necessary to understand which materials are better suited for specific applications, especially when considering real-world environmental factors such as humidity, pH changes, vibrations and impacts.
## Characterizing directly synthesized aCPs and aMOFs
Structural characterization is important to distinguish CPs from other metal–organic materials that lack a network-like nature (such as organic polymers decorated with metal ions). Characterization is also crucial for comparing the physicochemical properties of different classes of aCPs and for evaluating their performance in potential applications. We highlight five key features of aCPs (namely, the formation, amorphous structure, coordination state, stability and porosity) and discuss the techniques used to analyse these. We summarize experimental characterization methods herein (Table 2), but it should be noted that computer simulations (such as molecular simulations and coarse-grained modelling) can be used in conjunction with empirical data to provide structural insight. There are methods that can be used to specifically characterize aCPs, such as determining the pore size in liquids with fluorescent probes; however, many characterization methods can be used to study both aCPs and crystalline CPs. Although we focus on the characterization of directly synthesized aCPs, we also highlight when compositionally identical materials will give different results with certain characterization methods based on their short-range or long-range ordering.
## Direct formation
The formation of aCPs can be a challenging process to study owing to their typically rapid formation, and thus highly specialized equipment is generally required\(^{97,113}\). Additionally, as described earlier, many crystalline CPs transition from an amorphous state, and therefore studies examining the formation and crystallization of crystalline CPs sometimes are considered to have examined the formation of aCPs\(^{93}\). Formation can be observed through morphological changes using cryogenic or liquid cell transmission electron microscopy (TEM)\(^{89,126}\) and light scattering\(^{127,128}\), or by observing the molecular spacing and local environment using small-angle and wide-angle X-ray scattering and diffraction\(^{92,95,129,130}\), neutron scattering\(^{131}\), gravimetric analysis\(^{132}\), cryospray mass spectrometry\(^{133}\) and extended X-ray absorption fine
structure (EXAFS)\(^{93,134}\). In situ TEM can additionally allow for electron diffraction measurements to be taken at nearly the same time as imaging but requires customized equipment\(^{89,126}\). Similarly, EXAFS and small-angle X-ray scattering are generally performed using a synchrotron X-ray source and therefore often require access or collaboration with beamline scientists. Notably, EXAFS can effectively probe the chemical environment at the earliest stages of formation, which cannot be generally achieved with other X-ray techniques\(^{134}\). Finally, widely used techniques such as scanning electron microscopy (SEM)\(^{108,128,133}\) and ultraviolet–visible (UV–Vis) spectrophotometry\(^{30}\) can, respectively, enable monitoring of, for example, morphology changes and changes in turbidity\(^{129}\) or colour (ligand-to-metal charge transfer bands) to determine the formation and growth in solution or on substrates. Understanding the formation mechanism is important for engineering specific properties into aCPs. For example, it has been demonstrated that synthesizing MPNs from metal precursors that slowly transition into higher oxidation states, thereby slowing the formation of the MPN, produces thicker and more densely packed adherent aCPs on substrates than those formed directly from oxidized metal precursors\(^{33}\).
## Amorphous nature
The lack of long-range order in aCPs is relatively easy to confirm using various scattering techniques, but it can be challenging to study the short-range order and atomic positioning of the metals and ligands in aCPs. The techniques used to monitor the formation of aCPs can also be used to investigate the extent of their amorphous nature\(^{134}\). For example, the amorphous nature can be confirmed by X-ray diffraction, even though the absence of Bragg diffraction from aCPs inhibits the identification of atom positions\(^{104,106}\). However, the distribution of metal centres and the distances between atomic centres can be characterized by pair distribution function analyses, using a synchrotron X-ray source, to confirm the existence of metal–ligand–metal connectivity and to differentiate between crystalline CPs and aCPs in the same sample\(^{97,99,130}\). EXAFS can be used at different time points to demonstrate the time-dependent amorphous-to-crystalline transition of some CPs as this technique provides information about the metal–ligand distances and local configuration of atomic centres in aCPs and crystalline CPs\(^{93,134}\). The distribution of the organic linkers and the metals in aCPs can also be investigated using spin-diffusion
NMR spectroscopy owing to changes in the ligand signals (often \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \)) and changes to the metal signature (such as \( ^{27}\mathrm{Al} \), \( ^{31}\mathrm{P} \) and \( ^{113}\mathrm{Cd} \)) \( ^{65,135} \). Compared with the NMR spectra of crystalline materials, weaker metal peaks are often seen in the spectra of amorphous materials, and therefore NMR can be used to monitor the crystallization of aCPs even when \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) NMR shows no changes \( ^{136} \). Similarly, the peaks of aCPs in Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra tend to shift to lower wavenumbers compared with those of crystalline CPs owing to the generally disordered and weaker bonds in the amorphous networks \( ^{7} \). Electron microscopy such as TEM and SEM enables the observation of the morphology of aCPs, whereby the amorphous nature is suggested by the lack of defined edges \( ^{108,128,136} \). Moreover, electron diffraction can be performed in situ to confirm the amorphous nature. Thermogravimetric techniques can also be used to differentiate between crystalline CPs and aCPs, to some extent, because of the differences in thermal stability between crystalline and amorphous materials.
## Coordination states
The coordination state of aCPs has a notable influence on their physicochemical properties (such as the pH-responsiveness, mechanical robustness and the availability of free metal sites), which in turn influences their subsequent application \( ^{33,70,116} \). Understanding the coordination state is particularly relevant for some classes of aCPs that contain different chelation states in the same material (for example, MPNs can exhibit bis and tris complexes), and these states can often be characterized by fluorescence spectroscopy \( ^{137} \) or by monitoring the ligand-to-metal charge transfer bands using UV–Vis spectrophotometry \( ^{30,51,138} \). Moreover, shifts in binding energy as monitored by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and electron paramagnetic resonance spectroscopy can provide insight into the coordination state of the metal \( ^{78,139,140} \). NMR spectroscopy can be used to confirm coordination through the deprotonation of the organic ligand or through changes in the coordination number and geometry of the metal \( ^{135,136} \). FTIR spectroscopy can also demonstrate the deprotonation of the organic ligand \( ^{139} \) and is sensitive to the chelation state of phytate-based aCPs \( ^{141} \). More recently, it has been reported that the chelation complexes constituting aCPs have specific buffering capacity, and therefore titration can also be used to determine the coordination state \( ^{116} \). In most instances, the coordination state will help determine the overall stability of aCPs, which is an important factor that governs their performance in various applications.
## Stability
Many aCPs are stable in water, a trait that can be challenging to engineer into crystalline CPs \( ^{142} \). Studying their stability in different solvents, at different pH values, when exposed to different chelating agents and at different humidities and temperatures is crucial for understanding and applying aCPs in various fields. The coordination stability can be monitored with techniques similar to those used for studying the coordination state (such as UV–Vis spectrophotometry, NMR spectroscopy and FTIR spectroscopy). Notably, changes in the coordination state do not always correlate to disassembly in aCPs; for example, MPN capsules can remain intact with different coordination states \( ^{116} \). To study the overall stability of individual aCP particles, flow cytometry can be used to count the particles over time or in different conditions \( ^{30} \). Morphological characterization methods, such as atomic force microscopy \( ^{33} \), scattering \( ^{100} \) and optical and electron microscopy \( ^{38} \), can be used to monitor morphology changes and disassembly. For example, it was shown that iron–manganese-based aMOFs were stable
than their compositionally similar crystalline counterparts using SEM and X-ray diffraction \( ^{100} \). Alternatively, disassembly can be monitored directly through the metal or ligand content being released from the aCPs using techniques such as optical emission spectroscopy and mass spectrometry \( ^{38,69} \). Indirect methods can also be used to study disassembly, such as monitoring the release of encapsulated cargo \( ^{49,143} \). Additionally, thermogravimetric techniques can be used to study the stability of aCPs at high temperatures \( ^{58} \).
## Porosity
Several standard methods can be used to determine the porosity of aCPs either in gas, using adsorption–desorption analyses \( ^{120,138} \), or in solution, using permeability tests with polymeric probes such as fluorescein–dextran or small-molecule dyes \( ^{116} \). For example, the pores of azole-based aCPs are sufficiently large to be detected by \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption–desorption analysis, whereas the micropores of phenol or phosphonate systems are usually calculated by \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) uptake owing to their smaller size \( ^{33} \). Comparison of the pore sizes of aCPs (or aMOFs) and crystalline CPs (or crystalline MOFs) can provide insight into the origin of porosity; some amorphous materials have the same pore size as their crystalline counterparts, suggesting that in some cases, the pore size is derived from the precursor geometry regardless of the ordering \( ^{77} \). Unsaturated metal sites can strongly bind solvents, such that activation of the pores or removal of the solvent is not always possible \( ^{144} \) and, therefore, the porosity has to be probed in solution. In these instances, pore size can be investigated by using different solvents or by using molecular probes, which can allow for the identification of gating mechanisms and pH-dependent porosity \( ^{33,116} \). In addition, more advanced strategies such as cryo-TEM can be used to study the porous nature of aCPs. For example, with biomolecule-seeded aMOFs, the pores containing the biomolecule as well as those lacking the biomolecule could be visualized; Fourier transformation of the electron density scans enabled the accurate determination of pore sizes and distributions \( ^{82} \). Similarly, complex techniques such as positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy can be used to determine the pore sizes in aCPs \( ^{145} \), although this technique has rarely been used for directly synthesized aCPs.
## Future perspectives
The direct synthesis of aCPs has attracted interest because they demonstrate several unique material properties over their crystalline counterparts. The milder synthetic conditions (ambient temperature and pressure and/or free from organic solvents) can generate emergent types of materials, including adherent materials, free-standing films, glasses and templated replica materials using a wide range of metals and diverse ligands not commonly used for engineering crystalline materials. The amorphous structure also affords materials with tunable mechanical properties, exposed metal centres and greater processability, loading capacity and stability compared with their crystalline counterparts \( ^{146} \). These advantages suggest that aCPs might find use in various fields that require exposure to aqueous environments \( ^{38} \). For example, aCPs show particular promise in biomedical applications such as drug delivery, which involves exposure to complex media and biological triggers (such as the redox environment, pH, temperature and competitive chelators) \( ^{25,85,147} \). Moreover, the ability to readily construct aCPs from natural ligands and non-toxic metals reduces the risk of off-target effects and improves biodegradability, which are useful for biomedical and environmental applications \( ^{38,148,149} \).
A key advantage for future development is that the design and use of amorphous materials is not concerned with preserving delicate
crystal structures and, therefore, the future of aCPs is expected to be more expansive than for their crystalline counterparts. Although expanding the range of applicable ligands and metals is important for both crystalline CPs and aCPs, we emphasize two key aims for the future of aCPs, namely, improving the understanding of their formation and studying their processability and integration into macroscopic functional materials.
Identifying how defects, disorder and the continuous random network arise during aCP synthesis will allow for the rational design of amorphous structures and materials. This understanding is important, as it would enable more aCPs to be synthesized under aqueous ambient conditions, which in turn would lead to more aCPs that are stable in water72,100,142. In addition, this understanding will allow for better engineering of pores, exposed metal sites and side group accessibility of the ligands for separation and catalysis33 and might also provide insight into how to make crystalline CPs more water stable. Insight into their formation could also be useful in understanding crystalline materials and determining why crystalline CPs and aCPs with the same composition but prepared using different synthetic routes can have different properties.
aCPs are generally more adherent and more mechanically robust than their crystalline counterparts, and therefore processing aCPs and transforming them into functional macroscopic structures (such as thin films and monoliths) is more achievable. In principle, aCPs can be used from a liquid state, which would allow for more accurate monitoring of material properties (for example, brittleness and transparency) than is possible for the solid state and for creating more complex nanostructured materials. Moreover, the ability to readily use aCPs as coatings makes it easier to integrate them into composite materials than it is for crystalline materials; for example, aCPs have been used as protective coatings for biological cells150. Furthermore, exploring properties (such as the pore size, metal density and ligand length) of aCPs for the rational design of functional composite materials could provide unique opportunities for broad applications in diverse fields.
